@meta
id: 05-12
title: ge, the one people never learn
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-e, word-b
par: 23
minutes: 5
vscode: click just after the identifier
open: customer.provider.ts

@brief
Four motions cover three of the four possibilities:

    w   start of the next word        e   end of the next word
    b   start of the previous word    ?   end of the previous word

The missing one is `ge`. It walks backwards and lands on word *ends*,
which is precisely where you want to be when something needs appending to
a word you have already gone past — including anything sitting just
before a closing bracket, quote or semicolon.

`firstName` can carry stray whitespace from the import. Trim it. Line
three should read:

    const label = customer.firstName.trim() + customer.lastName;

`gE` is the WORD-sized version, exactly as you would expect. Either gets
you there.

@keys
| `ge` | back to the end of the previous word |
| `gE` | back to the end of the previous WORD |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `a` | append after the cursor |

@hints
The insertion point is the end of `firstName`, which is nearer the end of
the line than the start. So travel backwards — but `b` lands on starts,
and a start is not what you want.
--
`ge` is the backwards `e`. From the semicolon it visits the end of
`lastName`, the dot, the end of `customer`, the plus, and then the end of
`firstName`.
--
`2j$5ge` then `a`, type `.trim()`, `Esc`, `:wq`. Or `2j$2gE` if you would
rather count whitespace chunks — the same landing, three keys less
counting.

@solution
    2j $ 5ge  a.trim()<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

or

    2j $ 2gE  a.trim()<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`ge` is the least known of the six and it is worth the two keys it costs.
The situation it owns is appending to a token that already has something
after it — a closing paren, a comma, a semicolon. From `$` you are on the
punctuation; `i` would insert in front of the punctuation, which is
sometimes right, and `ge` puts you at the end of the actual word, which is
what you meant when the thing you are adding belongs to the identifier.

Compare the two routes in the solution. `5ge` counts every dot as a stop;
`2gE` counts whitespace chunks and gets there in less. That choice, made
fast and without thinking about it, is most of what this chapter is
teaching.

@start customer.provider.ts
export class CustomerProvider {
  public displayName(customer: Customer) {
    const label = customer.firstName + customer.lastName;

    return label.trim();
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
export class CustomerProvider {
  public displayName(customer: Customer) {
    const label = customer.firstName.trim() + customer.lastName;

    return label.trim();
  }
}
